{
	title: 'Terminal: full mode never clears scrollback by itself',
	spec: "In the terminal's full mode only a full redraw (a width change, a tab switch, Ctrl-L, a sliced history finishing its layout) clears screen and scrollback, because that clear snaps a scrolled-up terminal to the bottom. A frame shorter than the last one painted, while the last one was taller than the screen, gets blank rows inserted above the prompt area (at Frame.history) to keep its height; new rows fill them first, and a full redraw drops them. A changed row already in scrollback is not rewritten: it stays as it was until the next full redraw, and rows on the screen are rewritten in place. A paint that showed only the tail of a history still being laid out in slices (task 7j) is followed, once the layout finishes, by one full repaint, so scrollback holds the whole history. Scrollback and snapping follow different rules on the web, which is not affected.",
	status: 'done',
	notes: [
		"Invariant restored: in full mode, a bash call finishing never clears scrollback. snaps.jsonl showed '#tN (tool) 10 -> 5 rows, #tM (tool-result) 0 -> 4 rows' on nearly every call: 5 running output lines gave way to a 3-row glimpse. The layout cache (frame.state.history) keeps padded rows too, so a test of a forgotten mark must clear it as render.draw(true) does.",
		'2026-10-07: replaced the per-card height rule (frame.cardWater, b92c846) with the frame-wide one in render.paintParts: it covers tool calls, the prompt area shrinking and sent waiting messages too, and frame.ts went back to its 400-line limit. Before, a shrink or scrollback edit in full mode cleared screen and scrollback; snaps.jsonl showed it on nearly every bash call. The old Hal also cleared on both; it rarely shrank, since the prompt could only be edited scrolled to the bottom.'
	],
